#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecdaf760-2968-51ea-921d-5893dbb0fbb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/198f84781bcafa5560244ccf6e87df23a8d3e0ab621aaedb3c17df3fe1e52e31.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288065} {:answer/id #uuid "76ad1157-63d4-5359-84d7-4393d90a4dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/44740f82eec4457d2772aee7e292f1ab5f908723487a7f195a86a356ea5af810.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288066} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b90f28-5ea5-5270-ab42-0dc5760c1dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/6d66f5a7373826c316010328eaab1d6ac4cf64a82f9c7038b1f6f9450efd3388.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288067} {:answer/id #uuid "74f7b97e-896c-538c-b6a5-236b52d53497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/4df80bb9377923dfd59a686761c6dc3bd7517213f4da3f0e39401215ea22dbdd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288068} {:answer/id #uuid "b59db92c-e7bf-5c11-9227-1d9d4adf9ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/7c1114ff20331e5a1100523a460b8dddb017ea54389deb5f9388d16da4742d9e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76ad1157-63d4-5359-84d7-4393d90a4dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/44740f82eec4457d2772aee7e292f1ab5f908723487a7f195a86a356ea5af810.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288066} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e64751-a331-54a5-825b-fabe7c00029b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f594b9d5-1b7c-5654-b394-ec6bdd7e9748" :question/math-academy-id "q-105396" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-3,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/44740f82eec4457d2772aee7e292f1ab5f908723487a7f195a86a356ea5af810.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $g(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-3,1).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-3,1].$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $h(x)$, $p(x)$, and $q(x)$ are not continuous at some point that lies inside the interval $(-3,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57496436-8bd0-567e-8c37-ed584849ce1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8aed979-6828-5452-8c47-f9b99509f70d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288072} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3d70c5-345b-5d27-834c-a78acffee3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90(x-5)^{11}(y-2)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288073} {:answer/id #uuid "11ec50ed-3e27-55c9-ad05-62b94a27e393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288074} {:answer/id #uuid "b74ccb9b-cd4b-57a5-a4a0-33fb501e7699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288075} {:answer/id #uuid "778f6a04-cba3-5d0a-b75c-5ab69dca105b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60(x-5)^{11}(y-2)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8aed979-6828-5452-8c47-f9b99509f70d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288072} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd860098-0027-5c39-a5a3-2808bdcc7a1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08133ea2-e4bf-5c08-8db2-67322f4e831a" :question/math-academy-id "q-112230" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $5(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},$ $\\,6(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},$ and $12(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(5,6,12):$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,…,\\,55,\\,60,\\,65,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,\\,12,…,\\,54,\\,60,\\,66,…$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(5,6,12)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},$ and $(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2},$ we find the largest powers for $(x-5)$ and $(y-2)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x-5)^{5},(x-5)^{3},$ and $(x-5)^{3},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\n- For $(y-2)^{3},(y-2)^{3},$ and $(y-2)^{2},$ the largest power is $3.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x-5)$ and $(y-2)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},\\,(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},\\,(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2})=(x-5)^{5}\\cdot (y-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(5(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},\\,6(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},\\,12(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2}) & = \\\\ 60\\cdot (x-5)^{5}\\cdot (y-2)^{3} & = \\\\ 60(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ce52e6-f89f-553b-8e24-7079cde0acad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288079} {:answer/id #uuid "abc2b893-33c8-5d6f-b541-3795156fd5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288080} {:answer/id #uuid "a808053c-0a2e-5749-9579-6f15d0ed6659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288081} {:answer/id #uuid "877763b4-7bb3-595b-9419-5d46fc89bdc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288082} {:answer/id #uuid "f9bc7e49-2330-59ab-afb3-ddb72b27b2ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a808053c-0a2e-5749-9579-6f15d0ed6659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288081} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a804024-353d-51a6-ab20-6177b191b4a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "554b2923-f03c-51dd-9817-6e0a2f8280b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117799" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/c9ed24a7fa07d6c29e4125c8428fb61a42fc969b99f101c4737bdedf9bf036ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{4}+(-3)=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "644b7f09-7f66-50f2-8eba-ef6e009429d0" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient With Many Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b53f1e0c-9878-5edf-ae12-23b8ca09767d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288086} {:answer/id #uuid "f904d092-d8cf-59c1-9ca5-874e5e5565f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n+1}{{n}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288087} {:answer/id #uuid "3176c3d4-6baa-55c8-b986-604cc2198620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288088} {:answer/id #uuid "c565a5e6-196c-5d60-b35d-527b09f5280a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288089} {:answer/id #uuid "d73ec9b8-bdb8-5014-a198-16f0fc86068f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b53f1e0c-9878-5edf-ae12-23b8ca09767d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288086} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdfc674-38f1-53be-8547-04b940e6c071" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bb56c3d-ec97-553c-8bea-98b6d05242cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-19847" :question/problem "Given that $n\\ge 0$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n!)^{2}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(n+1)!=(n+1)n!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $n!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n!)^{2}}{((n+1)!)^{2}} & =\\frac{n!\\cdot n!}{(n+1)!\\cdot (n+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{n!}\\cdot \\cancel{n!}}{(n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4b5807f-a10a-518a-84fa-907bf699884c" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Functions With Vertical Reflections and Horizontal Shifts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a00f90a-ee29-5b3c-816d-2dc5523f4511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/2ebda1ba75caec9c62a9758696d2c4c45b452c29088e3811edfacd03acf3cd70.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288093} {:answer/id #uuid "fe91b53f-e376-5bfd-ae51-44942ea6aeb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/28e24035715baa30e89392408359033dc402264c0fdcdc5968addaa615aedf81.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288094} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd88fe1-3e9d-52e3-b5bd-ca9c372b50b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/be39498e5ad2c3148b254844722af707d721d7447a393a2341c6c0f6690c603e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288095} {:answer/id #uuid "c5487b96-5648-53cd-8412-d6f4288a834b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/b83ad0f69e6d4a61227c77b3dede3632accdceaa59c1bf63da8ca8bc3fe5a7f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288096} {:answer/id #uuid "fd94c884-7b43-50e8-947a-0fc7f75f7998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/01cc790392c233d00b4ab30e939230a0e7ac31708a0f568557a3e36e2cf04d67.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5487b96-5648-53cd-8412-d6f4288a834b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/b83ad0f69e6d4a61227c77b3dede3632accdceaa59c1bf63da8ca8bc3fe5a7f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288096} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe71d644-f1b1-570b-9374-0a70548f9d24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3efff02b-520d-5e70-beb3-91f843801d1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-28595" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{e}^{x}+5?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/f5983db4731c3fde0899ffc7afd602c7c82502f581742b62e69e8e6cf2d99e93.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{e}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/844a32e9d3640f9244b867c7411241171902c848defa0bf653b3820c3850238d.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $5$ units up to give $y=-{e}^{x}+5.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,4)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/c4cd8597702179629617890fce31272aa447a3227b74231e787a61eb4e770589.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f89affeb-c9cb-51a3-a416-c44398f9915a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f89affeb-c9cb-51a3-a416-c44398f9915a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288100} :answer-field/id #uuid "bff671db-ed68-5416-b881-dba2591e1dc2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e3f0e53-ded2-5798-9bc9-a98acc8b7d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340099" :question/problem "Given the function  $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{x}$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2}{x}+{e}^{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2+e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3619cf01-ea91-57d9-bbd0-7d43b8632f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288103} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1b2f61-ddae-55c2-b617-4d3ee295e617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-3)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288104} {:answer/id #uuid "d39c0330-fee5-507e-bdd1-da77637148f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-3)}^{2}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288105} {:answer/id #uuid "c5aa5192-d570-586c-98a6-48160e3e2557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288106} {:answer/id #uuid "699ce76e-3bd4-59ea-b31c-ae1693a193f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5aa5192-d570-586c-98a6-48160e3e2557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288106} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a22e649-9faf-54c1-94a0-495cebfb7361" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59a40bee-f512-5946-aafb-1fc0d88ab700" :question/math-academy-id "q-35081" :question/problem "Rewrite $3{q}^{2}-9q+15$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, we take out a factor of $3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n3({q}^{2}-3q+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-3q+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({q}^{2}-3q+5) & =3({(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\\cdot \\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb2e6993-5a38-593b-8c7e-69e37bc2b046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4(n + 1))/(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178590} {:answer/id #uuid "ef623fe7-d104-5668-a80d-eed8a003d95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2(n + 1))/(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178591} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9abc83-3521-5827-9a54-d009ddde269b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n + 4}{4n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178592} {:answer/id #uuid "667f15e0-1e00-5bdc-adf6-0445bbdaaa8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4n + 1}{4n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178593} {:answer/id #uuid "6f10c861-e6ca-5738-bfd4-cd746b9c8c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n + 4}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb2e6993-5a38-593b-8c7e-69e37bc2b046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4(n + 1))/(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178590} :answer-field/id #uuid "99924f7e-2d2a-59b8-9c5a-fe678a337911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65698589-f97d-56e6-8561-436b3f3f5011" :question/math-academy-id "q-41574" :question/problem "Write $4+\\frac{4}{n}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $4=\\frac{4}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $n$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n+4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(n+1)}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31de0804-798f-5962-84dc-f40d7bdb60bb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91605a00-67bd-5464-bc69-6904e8cd248c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288110} {:answer/id #uuid "8731f1cb-d519-5ea9-aec6-2e6f79d56fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288111} {:answer/id #uuid "e4056737-fba2-59d7-b21a-893dc1ad1a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288112} {:answer/id #uuid "d78aa272-1c7a-559e-8f5f-3924ec620d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288113} {:answer/id #uuid "0feefd77-5c9b-5260-b745-03ef8867e9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0feefd77-5c9b-5260-b745-03ef8867e9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288114} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ecdabf-507b-527a-9d1a-49143430ec08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6acbde6-eb5a-5b0e-a420-9d6912e79be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-59454" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/3303e36eb359692802a1c2184a2c54591f0b5de05853691841191a65689e8735.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph represents the motion of an object. Calculate the deceleration of the object." :question/worked-solution "The object is decelerating when its acceleration is negative. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line.\n\nThe line passes through the points $(30,10)$ and $(50,5),$ so its slope is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{5\\text{ m/s}-10\\text{ m/s}}{50\\text{ s}-30\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\text{ m/s}}{20\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =-0.25\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the object is $-0.25\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ which means that the deceleration of the object is $0.25\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb481f98-6670-5c5d-b81c-95cb68588410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{8} + \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203533} {:answer/id #uuid "ee41208f-bdef-5ddd-ad93-1d211a759e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2} + \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203534} {:answer/id #uuid "fa16cf43-e0b6-5e7a-a27b-8f6d2db2cd7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203535} {:answer/id #uuid "62af08cd-524a-574d-8cba-3514bb0957b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203536} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab67c8a-d85c-50fd-b84e-c8ea1717f0d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa16cf43-e0b6-5e7a-a27b-8f6d2db2cd7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203535} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9c7955-36b5-50a7-9966-50ed38493977" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae74ad8b-fb03-5739-8af6-2cdf099e6d37" :question/math-academy-id "q-65931" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef579077-27f7-5620-b706-9438a4dc43c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "437" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288117} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae99115-3fc7-5020-b104-70a0f93084c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "458" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288118} {:answer/id #uuid "451c4581-98c2-5c2a-b22c-139626372d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "416" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288119} {:answer/id #uuid "fd802a6f-accc-57f3-af3a-ae8f02c8ea9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "422" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288120} {:answer/id #uuid "c732a164-8941-53b8-bc94-9d291ecd4dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "462" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ae99115-3fc7-5020-b104-70a0f93084c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "458" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288118} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4ce2f7-054b-5767-b60d-2068602fa1f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "552a49a0-5903-5397-b618-440c26017861" :question/math-academy-id "q-67788" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/b0b100942a658966a5feea8a8677e7cfe86f21970a443385ff06034bbcb3d497.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ACD$ and $△ABC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006735/assets/7e3232258a523e4dbfebb6c380f5b88c2a54036b4d719e70ebf9060054f825d7.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ACD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 18\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =189\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈163.679,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△ABC:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 36\\cdot 30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{33}^{∘} \\\\  & =540\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{33}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈294.105,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ACD}+{A}_{ABC} \\\\  & =163.679+294.105 \\\\  & =457.784\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $458.$"}]}
